            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Case: The Email Received at 2 AM</h2>
<p>A 3C accessories overseas team in Shenzhen received a Google Ads account suspension notification at 2 AM. The subject line "Your account has been suspended due to suspicious payment activity" kept their campaign manager Zhang Ming (alias) awake all night. The next morning at 9 AM, he submitted his first version of appeal materials—a less than 100-word email with a business license and bank statement attached. Three days later, he received a rejection.</p>
<p>This is the real experience of many overseas businesses: the panic after account suspension leads to appeal materials that are either incomplete or miss the point entirely. This article uses this case as a breakdown to show you how to prepare appeal materials scientifically.</p>
<h2>Problem Diagnosis: First Figure Out "Why You Were Suspended"</h2>
<p>Google Ads account suspensions fall into three main categories:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Payment related</strong>: Suspicious payment activity, abnormal credit card binding, long-standing outstanding balance</li>
<li><strong>Policy violations</strong>: Counterfeit products, misleading content, sensitive categories (finance, healthcare, gambling, etc.)</li>
<li><strong>Fraudulent behavior</strong>: Invalid clicks, cash-out schemes, refund fraud</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Key Action</strong>: Before appealing, log into Google Ads backend and check the "Account Status" page for the specific suspension reason code. This code determines your appeal strategy and material focus.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Zhang Ming's team only discovered later that the real reason for suspension was <strong>using competitor brand names as keywords in ad materials</strong>, triggering Google's trademark policy—not the payment issue they initially suspected. This directly caused the "answering a different question" problem with their first appeal materials.</p>
<h2>Actions: The Correct Way to Prepare Appeal Materials</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Gather the "Evidence Chain"</h3>
<p>Appeal materials aren't simply explaining "I was wrong"—they need to provide documents that can **prove your innocence or demonstrate willingness to correct":</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Material Type</th>
<th>Specific Content</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Identity Proof</td>
<td>Business license (with notarized translation), Legal representative ID</td>
<td>Ensure company name matches Google Ads account</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Business Proof</td>
<td>Website URL, physical store/warehouse photos, Supply chain documentation</td>
<td>Prove business authenticity</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Remediation Statement</td>
<td>Specific violation analysis, List of executed modifications</td>
<td>Avoid empty apologies</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Historical Performance</td>
<td>Last 30 days ad data screenshots, Audience feedback records</td>
<td>Prove campaign management capability</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Step 2: Write an "Effective" Appeal Letter</h3>
<p>The appeal letter is the core of your materials. A higher success rate appeal letter should include:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Clearly state the violation type</strong>: Don't guess—directly cite the reason code from Google's suspension notice</li>
<li><strong>Sincerely acknowledge the issue</strong>: Explain which环节出了问题, not simply saying "we'll be more careful"</li>
<li><strong>Specific remediation measures</strong>: Provide verifiable modification plans, such as "deleted all ad groups containing competitor terms"</li>
<li><strong>Prevention mechanism</strong>: Explain how you'll avoid similar issues in the future, such as adding material review processes</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Negative Example</strong> (Zhang Ming's first appeal letter):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Dear Google team, our account has been suspended. Our company operates legitimately. Please help us unban. Thank you."</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Positive Example</strong> (Zhang Ming's rewritten appeal letter):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Regarding our account suspension due to policy violation (Reason code: REPUTATION_03), we have confirmed the issue: The landing pages contained unauthorized third-party brand names. We have executed the following remediation: 1. Deleted all violating ad groups; 2. Re-reviewed all landing pages; 3. Established a keyword filtering mechanism. To prevent similar issues, we have added secondary review processes before material launch. Please review."</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Step 3: Choosing the Submission Method</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Standard suspension</strong>: Submit through the "Request Review"入口 in Google Ads backend</li>
<li><strong>Sensitive categories or second appeals</strong>: Recommended to use the Google Ads official support form with more detailed documentation</li>
<li><strong>Urgent situations</strong>: If the account has high daily spend, try contacting your Google Ads account manager to expedite processing</li>
</ul>
<h2>Results: Different Outcomes After 72 Hours</h2>
<p>After resubmitting complete materials, Zhang Ming received account restoration notification within 48 hours. However, his peer—Li, who dealt in replica watches—was not so lucky. Li's account was suspended for <strong>clear trademark infringement</strong>. During appeal, he tried to explain with "legitimate purchase receipts," resulting in a second rejection.</p>
<p>Here's an important principle: <strong>For policy violation suspensions, appeal success rate depends on violation severity and history</strong>. Serious violations like counterfeit products or false advertising are difficult to appeal successfully even with complete materials.</p>
<h2>The "Second Half" After Appeal Approval</h2>
<p>Account restoration doesn't mean "everything is fine." Google's risk control system continuously monitors new accounts. Recommended actions:</p>
<ul>
<li>Reduce budget during the first two weeks after restoration to avoid triggering secondary review</li>
<li>Regularly check account "policy violation history" and promptly address potential risks</li>
<li>Enable two-factor authentication for important ad accounts and bind multiple administrators</li>
</ul>
<h2>In Conclusion</h2>
<p>There's no "universal template" for Google Ads account appeals, but there are "universal principles": Clarify why you were suspended, prepare materials that can prove your case, and write an appeal letter that the reviewer can "understand and be willing to help with." If you've tried 2-3 times yourself without success, consider professional intervention—time cost is often more expensive than service fees.</p>
